🌐 User Journey
A user types www.foobar.com in their browser.

The browser asks the DNS system to resolve www.foobar.com into an IP address.

The DNS record points www to 8.8.8.8, the IP of our single server.

The browser connects to that server over TCP/IP using HTTPS for secure communication.

The server responds with the website content.

🖥️ Infrastructure Components
Server → A physical or virtual machine hosting everything.

Domain name → foobar.com with a DNS record (www is a CNAME or A record) pointing to 8.8.8.8.

Web server (Nginx) → Handles HTTP/HTTPS requests, serves static files, and forwards dynamic requests to the application server.

Application server → Runs the business logic (e.g., PHP, Node.js, Python).

Application files (codebase) → The actual source code of the website.

Database (MySQL) → Stores and organizes persistent data (users, posts, transactions).

📌 Roles Explained
Domain name → Human‑friendly address that maps to the server’s IP.

DNS record www → Typically an A record pointing directly to 8.8.8.8.

Web server (Nginx) → Manages incoming HTTP requests, serves static content, and proxies dynamic requests.

Application server → Executes the codebase, generates dynamic responses.

Database (MySQL) → Provides structured storage and query capabilities.

Communication protocol → The server uses TCP/IP with HTTPS to securely deliver content to the user’s browser.

⚠️ Issues with This Infrastructure
SPOF (Single Point of Failure) → If the server goes down, the entire website is unavailable.

Downtime during maintenance → Restarting Nginx or deploying new code interrupts service.

No scalability → One server cannot handle heavy traffic; performance degrades quickly.

📝 Whiteboard Sketch (conceptual)
Imagine a box labeled Server (8.8.8.8). Inside it:

Nginx (web server) at the front.

Application server behind it, connected to:

Codebase (files) and MySQL database.
Outside:

DNS pointing www.foobar.com → 8.8.8.8.

User browser connecting via HTTPS.

